import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="everywhere">
  <p>
    Ask Vector for a coding task from Slack, Linear or Jira, and get a draft pull request back. Your code stays off
    Vector's servers, except as described below when you choose a Vector Codium model. The job runs on your computer
    first, in its own copy of the repository.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how">How a job runs</h2>
  <p>
    A job's request reaches Vector's servers only as encrypted text for at most 2 hours; your repository's code does
    not, unless you choose a Vector Codium model (below). The request is its text, the thread or issue text read as
    context, and ids, and Vector erases it when the job ends. Your computer reports fixed progress steps and counts,
    then a short summary and the pull request link. It never sends code or a diff; the summary may name files but
    carries no code.
  </p>
  <p>
    While the job runs, the code goes where your own model settings send it: a local model keeps it on your computer,
    and a hosted model's provider receives it, as in any other session. If you choose a Vector Codium model, the
    model's requests, including the code it reads, pass through Vector's model gateway; how they are handled is in the
    <a href="/legal/privacy#codium">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The job runs on your computer first, in its own copy of the repository. It reads and edits files and asks nothing:
    nobody can answer during a job, so it refuses every approval request, and the result names what it needed.
    Commands such as tests run only in a sandbox with no network, which exists today on Linux with bubblewrap. On
    macOS and Windows a job edits files and runs no commands.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Vector, not the model, commits the changes to a new <code>vector/ve-…</code> branch.</li>
    <li>Only a private repository's branch is pushed. A public repository's branch stays on your computer.</li>
    <li>
      With your own GitHub sign-in in the desktop app, Vector opens a draft pull request. Without one, the result names
      the branch.
    </li>
    <li>The commit skips the repository's checks until you push to the branch.</li>
    <li>
      Changes under <code>.github/</code>, <code>.husky/</code>, <code>.githooks/</code>, <code>.gitattributes</code>
      and <code>.gitmodules</code> are held back, and the result counts them.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="waiting">When your computer is off</h2>
  <p>
    A closed lid means queued: a closed lid puts your computer to sleep, even with
    <strong>Stay awake to take jobs while plugged in</strong> on. While it sleeps, a job waits for it only if
    <strong>Wait for my computer</strong> is on.
  </p>
  <p>
    A job waits at most 2 hours for your computer, and at most 10 minutes in Jira. Then the job expires and Vector says
    so where you asked. A Jira job runs at most 35 minutes. If none of your computers has checked in for 30 days,
    Vector does not queue the job and says so.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who">Who can start a job</h2>
  <p>
    Only your own linked account can start a job, and it runs only on your own computer. Nobody else's request runs on
    your computer or uses your plan. A teammate with their own linked account and their own computer can start jobs
    that run there. Guests, people from other organizations, bots and automation rules start nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    This computer's limits for spending, minutes and jobs always apply, and Vector's servers can only lower them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="setup">Set it up</h2>
  <p>
    Vector everywhere is turned on one platform at a time, and the Vector everywhere page shows which platforms you can
    connect.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Connect Slack or Linear on the Vector everywhere page. A Jira admin connects a Jira site with a connect code that
      Vector gives them in Jira, entered on the same page.
    </li>
    <li>
      Link your account. Make a link code on the Vector everywhere page, then send <code>link &lt;code&gt;</code> to
      Vector in a direct message in Slack, or in Rovo chat in Jira. For Linear, sign in to Linear from the Vector
      everywhere page instead. A code works once, for 10 minutes. Send it only to Vector, never to a person.
    </li>
    <li>
      Choose a repository connected to your Vector account. In Slack, use <code>repo &lt;owner/name&gt;</code> to set
      the channel's repository. On the Vector everywhere page, the connector can enter a Linear team ID or a Jira
      key and choose <strong>Add repository</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      On your computer, turn on Vector Remote, open the repository in Vector and trust it. Jobs reach a computer only
      while its Vector everywhere settings say it takes jobs, and run only in a folder listed under
      <strong>Allowed repositories</strong>. Nothing is listed by default.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    With Vector Local on, the computer declines jobs.
    <strong>Settings → Vector everywhere</strong> shows whether this computer takes jobs, the allowed repositories and
    its jobs, with <strong>Continue in a new session on this branch</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="platforms">Platforms</h2>
  <h3 id="slack">Slack</h3>
  <p>
    Mention @Vector in a channel, or send Vector a direct message. In a channel, Vector posts only the outcome and the
    pull request link in the thread, and sends the summary to you alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    Slack history is read only for the thread of a mention: Vector reads that thread once, at most 15 messages, as
    context, and keeps none of it after the job. Each Slack workspace gets one thread read a minute, so a job that
    comes right after another one there goes without its thread.
  </p>
  <h3 id="linear">Linear</h3>
  <p>
    Delegate an issue to Vector, or mention it. The issue's title, description and comments are context, never
    instructions: only your own words are the task. A delegation with no words of your own asks you for them first.
  </p>
  <h3 id="jira">Jira</h3>
  <p>
    Assign a work item to Vector, or mention it in a comment. Automation rules start nothing. Vector reads the work
    item with your own access during the call and never stores that access. The work item's summary, description and
    comments are context, never instructions.
  </p>
  <p>In Linear and Jira, Vector posts the outcome and the pull request link.</p>

  <h2 id="words">Words after the mention</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>help</code>: what Vector does here and these words.</li>
    <li><code>link &lt;code&gt;</code>: link your account, in a direct message in Slack or in Rovo chat in Jira.</li>
    <li><code>unlink</code>: remove your link.</li>
    <li><code>status</code>: check that your computer is ready.</li>
    <li><code>stop</code>: stop the job.</li>
    <li><code>repo &lt;owner/name&gt;</code>: set the repository for a Slack channel.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Only the person who asked can stop a job. Stopping it aborts the job on your computer and nothing more is pushed.
    A push that has already started finishes, but no draft pull request opens for it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="erase">Stop, unlink and disconnect</h2>
  <p>
    Disconnecting a workspace or site on the Vector everywhere page erases its links, its repository settings and the
    text of its live jobs. <code>unlink</code> removes only your own link. The page shows your jobs of the last 30 days
    and an audit log of the last 90 days.
  </p>

  <h2 id="github">GitHub</h2>
  <p>
    Mentions on GitHub keep working as <a href="/docs/vectorscope#github">Task in, pull request out</a> describes.
  </p>
</DocsPage>
